# Shell scripts and git hooks must keep LF endings regardless of core.autocrlf.
# With CRLF, `set -euo pipefail` is parsed as `pipefail\r` and bash aborts with
# "invalid option name" — the hook and the publish script silently stop working
# on a Windows clone.
*.sh        text eol=lf
scripts/hooks/*  text eol=lf

# Lua sources too, because `.stylua.toml` sets `line_endings = "Unix"` and a
# formatter's rule that the working tree contradicts is a rule that fails only
# for some contributors. With `core.autocrlf=true` on Windows, a checked-out
# `.lua` file arrives CRLF, `stylua --check` rejects every line of it, and the
# same command passes on Linux CI — which is the worst shape a check can have:
# green where nobody is looking, red where the work happens.
*.lua       text eol=lf

# Markdown/vimdoc too — `scan.lua` reads these to build the map's own "docs"
# cross-reference index (`refs` counts, backlinks), which is line-oriented
# and pattern-matches per line. A `\r` left on the end of every line by a
# CRLF checkout changes what those patterns see there, so a Windows-edited
# `.md` produces a different `refs` count than the same content checked out
# with LF — silently, since nothing here reads raw bytes the way the map's
# HTML/JSON diff would catch a byte mismatch directly. Found exactly this
# way: `docs/COMMANDS.md`, edited on Windows, made `--check` report the map
# stale for a reason that had nothing to do with the map being wrong.
*.md        text eol=lf
*.txt       text eol=lf

# Generated map artifacts: keep them out of diffs and language stats, but do
# not mark them binary — they are reviewed on purpose.
#
# `eol=lf` for the same reason as the two blocks above, and here it is not a
# tidiness argument but a correctness one: `--check` regenerates these in
# memory and **byte-compares** them against what is on disk. The generator
# writes LF. With `core.autocrlf=true` on Windows they are checked out as CRLF,
# every byte after the first newline differs, and the `map` gate reports the
# map as stale forever — on a clean checkout, with nothing actually wrong.
# Regenerating appears to fix it until the next `git checkout` or `git stash`
# converts them back.
docs/map/index.html      text eol=lf linguist-generated=true
docs/map/module_map.json text eol=lf linguist-generated=true
docs/map/overview.md     text eol=lf linguist-generated=true

# Generated from the KEYS table and the two manifests by `scripts/gen_map.lua`.
# Not byte-compared by `--check`, but it is written by the same run, so a CRLF
# working copy would show it as modified after every regeneration.
docs/BINDINGS.md         text eol=lf linguist-generated=true
